  assign comp_pitches = 'A nice low-key one for upper primary writers. $100 prize plus the winning story published on the Book Links website. Open theme.|NT kids build a bug from pre-used materials and explain where it lives, or make a Crocwise poster. Top 3 in every category at every year level win cash.|Nearly $20,000 in prizes, all paid towards musical equipment or tuition. A breakthrough comp for any kid who writes songs in their bedroom.|Tasmanian kids from Kinder to Year 12 decode the universe with a picture book, photo essay, video or invention. Kinder to Year 2 gets its own division.|Run by SA’s Commissioner for Aboriginal Children and Young People. First Nations kids’ art on the Children’s Day theme is displayed during NAIDOC Week.|One original poem, any Australian school kid. Winner takes $1,000 and their poem joins performances seen by over 100,000 students. Poet keeps copyright.|For 25 years, teachers across Northern Tasmania have sent in student art. Selected pieces are hung, professionally presented, in a real museum gallery.|Frog conservation through art. Open to all ages and free to enter, so Aussie kids are welcome. Quirky, niche and surprisingly popular.|Kids design and run their own investigation, then share what they found on a digital poster. Cash prizes per division, plus a sustainability stream.|The youth arm of Flickerfest, Australia’s Academy Award qualifying short film festival. Winning films screen at Bondi beside the international lineup.|The winning drawing becomes the real national Children’s Week poster, printed and sent to schools, libraries and councils around the country.|A small US festival comp that now draws entries from dozens of countries. A perfect home for kids who love owls. Original art only, posted to Minnesota.|Going since 1924. The ACA picks a winner from the cartoons kids send in to theINmag each issue. The winner gets a year of Inkspot, their own magazine.|Camp Australia runs a national art comp every term. Past prizes include hoodies printed with kids’ own art and a trophy designed by The Brickman himself.|Crayola’s year-round open call. Less about winning, more about joining a global creative movement. Kids share what they make, tagged #StayCreative.|Not a competition, a challenge. Read books from your state’s list and earn a certificate signed by the Premier. A big motivator for reluctant readers.|Your drawing, story or comic on a real printed page, in the hands of thousands of kids across Australia. Send in anything you’ve made, any time. Send it IN.|Australia’s biggest creativity showcase, running since 1869. Hundreds of junior classes, and winning entries hang in the Arts & Crafts Pavilion.|Australia’s coolest under-the-radar youth film festival. Selected films premiere in Hobart, then tour nationally. Phone footage is fine.|For kids who light up at hard maths problems. Four contests, five questions each, no calculator. More about how creatively you got there than the answer.|Run by Children’s Rights Queensland with the Queensland Family and Child Commission. Age-group winners shared over $2,000 in prizes in 2025.|Grab a free colouring sheet at any Eckersley’s store or online, colour it in however you like, and winners take home $50 Eckersley’s gift cards.|Faber-Castell and Cool Australia set a classroom brief each year. The school behind the most inspiring entries scores $1,500 of Faber-Castell products.|Where kids fall in love with engineering. Teams build a LEGO robot to solve missions, then compete from regional level all the way to international.|Aussie-owned art supplies brand Micador runs regular giveaways like Win a Year of Micador, with ranges for toddlers right through to tweens.|Run by the NSW Advocate for Children and Young People, with themes built around children’s rights. Group entries are welcome, three age categories.|State championships leading to a National Final for young piano, strings, drums and guitar players. Top placers play State Finals at iconic venues.|Kids draw a Christmas card front cover and the winning design is printed on actual Smiggle Christmas cards. Prizes usually include vouchers and Smiggle gear.|Staedtler’s annual high school comp with a $5,000 prize pool, judged by three professional Australian artists. Weekly draws give away $200 art packs.|Kids send in a drawing on the year’s theme, and for every picture Staedtler donates to a Plan International project supporting girls’ education.|Entered as a school. Over 10,000 students a year tell a story through dance, drama and music in professional theatres. Storytelling beats technique.|Run by Marion City Council’s library network. First prize up to $200, second up to $100. Word limits 300-750 (9-10s) or 500-1,000 (11-13s). Open theme.|The little sibling of the Archibald. Kids paint or draw a portrait of someone who matters to them, and finalists hang in the Art Gallery of NSW.|Run by ANSTO, Australia’s nuclear science organisation. Free to enter, with generous prizes for winning schools. Particularly strong at primary level.|A regional WA gem. Kids get a 30x30cm canvas to paint, and finalists hang alongside the famous Cossack Art Awards. The overall winner takes home $300.|One of Australia’s longest-running kids’ poetry comps, named for the writer of My Country. Cash prizes, plus an annual anthology of the best poems.|Goulburn Valley kids write a short story or a poem on any subject. First prize in each category is $300, judging is blind, and the author keeps copyright.|The classic spoken spelling bee for NSW Years 3 to 6. Kids progress from class to school to regionals, with State Finals held at Penrith.|Run by the NSW Department of Education with the State Library. Winning stories are published in the Best of WriteOn anthology, kids’ words in print.|Since 1952, Victorian kids have entered a picture book or an invention in this science comp. Prep to Year 12, tens of thousands in bursaries, all sections.|Run by Aussie stationery brand Spencil. Major prize packs worth $400 each are stuffed with art supplies, so the prize is fuel for the next masterpiece.|Run by Perth’s kids’ lit festival, Scribblers. The overall winner takes home an e-reader and book pack, and shortlisted writers get book subscriptions.|Run by the CYA Conference for children’s and young adult writers. Cash prizes of $100, $60 and $40, with categories across different writing forms.|Run by the City of Sydney. Kids capture the city through their own lens, phone snaps welcome, and selected photos exhibit outdoors in central Sydney.|Cash prizes for prose and poetry from primary writers in WA’s Midwest and Gascoyne. Winners get a perpetual trophy displayed at their school.|Named after picture book legend Shaun Tan. Winning art hangs for a month at Subiaco Library, professionally framed, with the City picking up the tab.|Cash prizes for first, second and third in every age category, Grades 3 to 12. Any subject at all, with word limits that step up by year level.|Run by Australian Science Innovations. Fifty minutes of problem solving in real-life contexts, and a pathway into their science extension programs.|Run by Boroondara Library Service in Melbourne since 1999. The young writers categories share over $5,000 in prize money, huge for a council award.|A small-but-mighty comp from the Rocky River Writers Club in country SA, with prose and poetry streams. Note: entries must be posted, not emailed.|ACT school kids draw an A4 poster on the year’s Reconciliation Day theme. The winning school takes $1,000 and the poster can become a public mural.|Shortlisted writers score a one-on-one workshop with a published local author, and finalists are published in the SPYWA Anthology. Prizes up to $150.|The Whitlam Institute asks kids one question, what matters? Answer however you want. Top prize is $1,500 and every entry is read by a real human.|Aussie kids’ clothing brand Banabae prints the winner’s art onto real clothes in their next collection. The winner also takes home a $500 voucher.|Make a real book, cover to cover: original story, original illustrations, hand-bound, solo or in a duo. Run by the Children’s Book Council of Australia (WA).|Run by Australia Zoo and the Irwin family. Top prize is two nights at the Crocodile Hunter Lodge, worth over $1,800. Robert Irwin started out here.|A five-day online maths comp where participation comes first. Top schools win up to $1,500 and every student gets a certificate.|Entered through NSW schools. Fifty pieces of student art are gifted to The Children’s Hospital at Westmead every single year.|Run by the Australian Conservation Foundation. Kids make art about an Aussie threatened species, and judges look for heart over technical skill.|Australia’s oldest and biggest maths comp, entered through schools since 1978. Every student gets an award, and the Olympiad pathway starts here.|Prep to Year 6 kids get a division all their own. Queensland teams across 13 regions take seven weeks on a big challenge with zero outside help.|A national spelling tradition for Years 3 to 8. Kids compete from school to a national final, and past winners have met the actual Prime Minister.|Australia’s largest junior art, craft and design display. Real prize money and real exhibition space in the Goyder Pavilion, from painting to pottery.|Teams get six weeks on one big problem with zero adult help, then perform their solution to a live audience and face a surprise challenge on the day.|Founded by SAP and free to enter, with about $30,000 in cash prizes each year. Kids build real tech projects, from coding apps to building robots.|Run by Sydney bookstore Better Read Than Dead. Every entry is published in an anthology, so just by entering, kids become published writers.|NT kids write and make a real book, cover to cover. The Young Territory Author of the Year takes $500, and entries join a Darwin library collection.|A state-by-state comp for young composers, from solo instrument to rock, pop and EDM. Winning compositions are performed live at the awards ceremony.|Draw a design on the theme Art in Bloom. Annette Blair blows the winning design in glass live at Floriade, and the finished piece goes home with the kid.|Run by Victorian water corporations. Kids design a poster on the year’s water theme, and category winners progress to state finals in National Water Week.|In its 20th year. Gladstone region primary kids make art about the Australia they care about, and chosen pieces travel to sister city Saiki in Japan.|The comp Robert Irwin won as a kid. Run by the SA Museum with Australian Geographic. Junior winners take home $500 and every finalist gets exhibited.' | split: '|'
